WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.222
Statistical inference uses sample evidence to learn beyond the observed data

00:00:05.222 --> 00:00:11.797
If thirty of fifty sampled people support a proposal, the sample proportion is sixty percent

00:00:11.797 --> 00:00:17.933
Whether that estimates a wider population depends on how those people entered the sample.

00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:26.771
Under independent binary sampling with a common success probability, we can estimate how a sample proportion varies

00:00:26.771 --> 00:00:32.092
Here the estimated standard error is about zero point zero six nine

00:00:32.092 --> 00:00:38.300
The model supplies an uncertainty scale; the observed proportion alone cannot show its precision.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.300
Random assignment answers a different question from random sampling

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:51.089
Assigning volunteers randomly to treatments helps separate treatment effects from preexisting group differences

00:00:51.089 --> 00:00:59.967
An observed success-rate difference of three tenths still carries sampling uncertainty; assignment does not eliminate random variation.

00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:09.083
Random sampling supports population generalization, while random assignment supports causal comparison under suitable implementation

00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:12.992
Neither is replaced by a complicated formula

00:01:12.992 --> 00:01:23.600
State the population, design, assumptions, effect estimate, and uncertainty so the conclusion remains matched to the evidence actually collected.
